{"results":[{"url":"https://www.facebook.com/NPR/posts/fact-check-president-trump-claimed-that-us-carbon-emissions-are-down-thats-false/10159952558591756/","final_url":"https://www.facebook.com/NPR/posts/fact-check-president-trump-claimed-that-us-carbon-emissions-are-down-thats-false/10159952558591756/","title":"NPR","description":"Fact Check: President Trump claimed that U.S. carbon emissions are down. That's false.\n\nTotal emissions have continued to increase during his tenure — and his administration has rolled back a host of...","language":"en","text":"Fact Check: President Trump claimed that U.S. carbon emissions are down. That's false.\n\nTotal emissions have continued to increase during his tenure — and his administration has rolled back a host of Obama-era regulations to reduce greenhouse gases.\n\nAll reactions:\n\n1.7K comments\n\n2.7K shares\n\nLike\n\nComment\n\nMost relevant\n\n\n\nBill Mohr\n\nIf they have declined any since the peak of his administration, I am sure that the pandemic has had it's impact as well as people going ahead with alternatives in spite of the administrations attempts to thwart clean energy. But -- if you include the wildfires then perhaps the atmospheric CO2 release from the US has increased in spite of what was previously stated.\n\nJon Bewall\n\nIt's difficult to understand why folks believe anything he says, with the encompassing ignorance he displays. You'd be better served by believing the opposite, every time.\n\n2\n\nMargaret Hofstetter\n\nBut it all sounds so great to his supporters who basically want to live in the past. \"Make America Go Back in Time So We Don't Have to Deal with the Reality of Where We Are Now\" should be the slogan (it's a little long). . .\n\n8\n\nView all 2 replies\n\nDaniel P. B. Smith\n\nTrump's promises to rescue coal jobs were fantasy and empty rhetoric. When we were passing through Gillette, Wyoming in 2016, we had an chance to tour the Eagle Butte coal mine, but we were in a hurry to get to Yellowstone and couldn't quite squeeze it in. We missed our chance. Both of the coal mines in Gillette went bankrupt and closed in 2019. On Donald Trump's watch. After three years of his energy policy.\n\n13\n\nView all 3 replies\n\nMarybeth Gex MacDowell\n\nI’m all for helping the environment. However, as Trump said last night, finally, if China, India, Russia etc, don’t get on board its kind of a losing battle. We have gone from buying bottled water to using two Britas. Works great! One small thing. But something!\n\nView 1 reply\n\nJanet Winchell\n\nSo easy to say but once again No Facts just words; why don’t you have the citations from the authorities who do the testing so the public can go to the source and read it? Could you imagine lawyers writing a legal brief without legal citations to confirm the law?\n\nCyndle Clift\n\nGunna fact check joe? Gunna report on joe and Ukraine, China, Russia and Kazakhstan? You are loosing credibility with your listeners.\n\n1\n\nView all 2 replies\n\nDaniel Reasoner\n\nNo joe fact checks? Just for trump? Reporting on the debate or reporting on Trump? One sided reporting or impartial journalism? Anything in the next 18 days would be great. After that it's just reporting on whatever gets you clicks.\n\n6\n\nJames Bovell\n\nA magnificent victory for trump. This debate shows beyond doubt that Americans have only one and one viable choice and that TRUMP. Biden is ill . Ans corrupt and ill suited to deal with the presidency\n\n26\n\nView all 11 replies\n\nStephanie Stander\n\nOld man obviously losing his grip on reality rants about windmills and the threats they pose. Don Quixote or presidential debate, pretty sad when they’re echoing the same themes.\n\n2","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":3666.547189999619,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}